# Skillora Family Mobile - Cursor AI Rules

## Project Overview
This is a Flutter mobile application for parents and students in the Skillora ecosystem. The app follows Clean Architecture principles with feature-based organization, using Riverpod for state management and GoRouter for navigation.

## Critical Rules

### Documentation Files
- **NEVER create markdown (.md) files unless explicitly requested by the user**
- Do not generate README, documentation, or any .md files unless the user specifically asks for them
- Focus on code implementation, not documentation

### Architecture & Code Organization

#### Clean Architecture Layers
- **Presentation Layer**: UI screens, widgets, Riverpod consumers (`lib/features/*/presentation/`)
- **Domain Layer**: Business models, entities, use cases (`lib/core/domain/`, `lib/features/*/domain/`)
- **Data Layer**: Repositories, data sources, API services (`lib/core/data/`, `lib/features/*/data/`)

#### Feature Structure
When creating a new feature, follow this structure:
```
feature_name/
├── data/
│   ├── feature_repository.dart    # Repository interface/implementation
│   └── feature_model.dart          # Data models (if feature-specific)
├── domain/
│   └── feature_model.dart          # Domain models (immutable, with copyWith)
├── presentation/
│   ├── feature_screen.dart         # Main screen widget
│   └── feature_widgets.dart        # Feature-specific widgets (if needed)
└── providers/
    └── feature_providers.dart       # Riverpod providers (if needed)
```

#### Core Directory Usage
- Shared code goes in `lib/core/`
- Domain models in `lib/core/domain/`
- Reusable widgets in `lib/core/widgets/` or `lib/core/ui/components/`
- Theme/tokens in `lib/core/theme/`
- Services in `lib/core/data/services/`
- Providers in `lib/core/providers/`

## State Management with Riverpod

### Provider Patterns
- Use `StateNotifierProvider` for complex state with business logic
- Use `Provider` for simple state and dependency injection
- Use `FutureProvider` or `StreamProvider` for async data
- Always use `ConsumerWidget` or `ConsumerStatefulWidget` in presentation layer
- Derive providers from other providers when possible (e.g., `currentUserProvider` from `authProvider`)

### State Notifier Pattern
```dart
// State class (immutable)
class FeatureState {
  final List<Item> items;
  final bool isLoading;
  final String? error;
  
  const FeatureState({
    this.items = const [],
    this.isLoading = false,
    this.error,
  });
  
  FeatureState copyWith({...}) { ... }
}

// StateNotifier
class FeatureNotifier extends StateNotifier<FeatureState> {
  FeatureNotifier(this._ref) : super(const FeatureState());
  final Ref _ref;
  
  Future<void> loadItems() async {
    state = state.copyWith(isLoading: true, error: null);
    try {
      // ... logic
    } catch (e) {
      state = state.copyWith(isLoading: false, error: e.toString());
    }
  }
}

// Provider
final featureProvider = StateNotifierProvider<FeatureNotifier, FeatureState>((ref) {
  return FeatureNotifier(ref);
});
```

### AsyncValue Pattern
When using FutureProvider, handle AsyncValue properly:
```dart
final dataProvider = FutureProvider<List<Item>>((ref) async {
  final repository = ref.watch(repositoryProvider);
  return repository.getItems();
});

// In UI
dataProvider.when(
  data: (items) => ItemList(items: items),
  loading: () => CircularProgressIndicator(),
  error: (error, stack) => ErrorWidget(error: error),
)
```

## Navigation with GoRouter

### Route Definition
- Use `GoRoute` for simple routes
- Use `StatefulShellRoute.indexedStack` for bottom navigation tabs
- Always provide route names for type-safe navigation
- Use path parameters for dynamic routes: `path: '/item/:id'`

### Navigation Methods
- Use `context.go('/path')` for navigation (replaces current route)
- Use `context.push('/path')` for stack navigation
- Use `context.goNamed('route_name', pathParameters: {...})` for type-safe navigation
- Pass data via `state.extra` when needed

### Route Guards
- Implement authentication checks in router's `redirect` callback
- Check `ref.read(isAuthenticatedProvider)` for auth state

## Domain Models

### Model Requirements
- All domain models must be immutable (use `final` fields)
- Always implement `copyWith` method for state updates
- Include `fromJson` and `toJson` factory methods
- Use enums for status/type fields (e.g., `UserRole`, `ClassStatus`)
- Provide computed getters for derived values

### Example Model Pattern
```dart
class ItemModel {
  final String id;
  final String title;
  final ItemStatus status;
  final DateTime createdAt;
  
  const ItemModel({
    required this.id,
    required this.title,
    required this.status,
    required this.createdAt,
  });
  
  ItemModel copyWith({
    String? id,
    String? title,
    ItemStatus? status,
    DateTime? createdAt,
  }) {
    return ItemModel(
      id: id ?? this.id,
      title: title ?? this.title,
      status: status ?? this.status,
      createdAt: createdAt ?? this.createdAt,
    );
  }
  
  factory ItemModel.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) { ... }
  Map<String, dynamic> toJson() { ... }
  
  // Computed getters
  bool get isActive => status == ItemStatus.active;
  String get displayName => title;
}
```

## Logging

### AppLogger Usage
- **ALWAYS** use `AppLogger` instead of `print()` statements
- Use appropriate log levels:
  - `AppLogger.verbose()` - Very detailed debugging (debug only)
  - `AppLogger.debug()` - Development debugging (debug only)
  - `AppLogger.info()` - General application flow
  - `AppLogger.warning()` - Potentially problematic situations
  - `AppLogger.error()` - Runtime errors, API failures
  - `AppLogger.fatal()` - Critical errors causing crashes

### Logging Patterns
```dart
// With context
AppLogger.info('User logged in', context: {
  'userId': user.id,
  'email': AppLogger.sanitize(user.email),
});

// With error
AppLogger.error('Failed to load data', error: e, stackTrace: stackTrace);

// API logging (use AppLogger.logApiRequest/Response in ApiClient)
```

### PII Sanitization
- Always sanitize sensitive data: `AppLogger.sanitize(email)`
- Never log passwords, tokens, or full API keys
- Use `AppLogger.sanitize()` for emails, phone numbers, tokens

## Error Handling

### Exception Handling
- Use custom `ApiException` for API errors
- Always catch exceptions and provide user-friendly error messages
- Log errors with full context: `AppLogger.error(..., error: e, stackTrace: stackTrace)`
- Use `AppToast.showApiError()` for user-facing error messages

### Error Handling Pattern
```dart
try {
  final result = await repository.getData();
  // Handle success
} on ApiException catch (e, stackTrace) {
  AppLogger.logApiError(
    url: url,
    error: e.message,
    statusCode: e.statusCode,
    stackTrace: stackTrace,
  );
  AppToast.showApiError(statusCode: e.statusCode, message: e.message);
  // Handle error state
} catch (e, stackTrace) {
  AppLogger.error('Unexpected error', error: e, stackTrace: stackTrace);
  // Handle unexpected error
}
```

## API Integration

### ApiClient Usage
- Always use `ApiClient` from `lib/core/data/api_client.dart`
- Use service classes (e.g., `UserService`, `ClassService`) that wrap `ApiClient`
- Services should be provided via Riverpod providers
- Always handle API errors gracefully

### Service Pattern
```dart
class FeatureService {
  final ApiClient _client;
  final String _baseUrl;
  
  FeatureService(this._client, this._baseUrl);
  
  Future<List<Item>> getItems() async {
    try {
      final response = await _client.getList('/items');
      return response.map((json) => ItemModel.fromJson(json)).toList();
    } catch (e, stackTrace) {
      AppLogger.logApiError(url: '$_baseUrl/items', error: e, stackTrace: stackTrace);
      rethrow;
    }
  }
}

final featureServiceProvider = Provider<FeatureService>((ref) {
  final apiClient = ref.watch(apiClientProvider(ApiConfig.featureServiceBaseUrl));
  return FeatureService(apiClient, ApiConfig.featureServiceBaseUrl);
});
```

### Repository Pattern
- Repositories abstract data sources (can be API or mock data)
- Repositories are provided via Riverpod
- Currently supports `AssetDataProvider` (mock) and API services

## UI & Theming

### Design System Usage
- **ALWAYS** use design tokens from `lib/core/theme/tokens.dart`:
  - Colors: `AppColors.*` (e.g., `AppColors.primary`, `AppColors.brandTeal`)
  - Spacing: `AppSpacing.*` (e.g., `AppSpacing.md`, `AppSpacing.xl`)
  - Border Radius: `AppRadii.*` (e.g., `AppRadii.md`, `AppRadii.sm`)
  - Typography: Use `AppTypography` from theme

### Theme Access
- Use `Theme.of(context)` to access theme data
- Use `Theme.of(context).colorScheme.*` for Material 3 colors
- Use `Theme.of(context).textTheme.*` for typography

### Reusable Components
- Use existing components from `lib/core/ui/components/`:
  - `AppButton` - For buttons
  - `AppCard` - For cards
  - `AppListTile` - For list items
  - `AppSectionHeader` - For section headers
  - `InfoBanner` - For info messages

### Widget Patterns
- Use `ConsumerWidget` or `ConsumerStatefulWidget` for widgets that need Riverpod
- Extract complex widgets into private classes (e.g., `_FeatureSection`)
- Use `const` constructors where possible for performance
- Implement proper loading, error, and empty states

### UI State Handling
```dart
// Always handle AsyncValue states
dataProvider.when(
  data: (data) => DataWidget(data: data),
  loading: () => const CircularProgressIndicator(),
  error: (error, stack) => ErrorWidget(error: error),
)

// Or with ConsumerWidget
final data = ref.watch(dataProvider);
if (data.isLoading) return CircularProgressIndicator();
if (data.hasError) return ErrorWidget(error: data.error);
return DataWidget(data: data.value!);
```

## Type Safety

### Null Safety
- Always use null-safe operators (`?`, `!`, `??`)
- Provide default values where appropriate
- Use `const` for immutable collections
- Use `final` for variables that don't change

### Type Definitions
- Use enums for status/type fields
- Use sealed classes or union types when appropriate
- Avoid `dynamic` - use proper types or `Object?`

## Code Quality

### Naming Conventions
- Files: `snake_case.dart` (e.g., `user_profile.dart`, `home_screen.dart`)
- Classes: `PascalCase` (e.g., `UserProfile`, `HomeScreen`)
- Variables/Functions: `camelCase` (e.g., `userName`, `getUserData`)
- Constants: `camelCase` with `const` (e.g., `const defaultTimeout`)
- Private members: `_leadingUnderscore` (e.g., `_privateMethod`, `_privateField`)

### Code Organization
- One class per file (except for related private classes)
- Group imports: Flutter packages, then project imports
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Keep functions small and focused
- Extract complex logic into separate methods

### Documentation
- Add doc comments for public APIs
- Use `///` for documentation comments
- Document complex business logic
- Add TODO comments for future improvements

## Role-Based Features

### Role Checking
- Always check user role: `ref.watch(isParentProvider)`, `ref.watch(isStudentProvider)`
- Use `ref.watch(authProvider)` to access current user and selected child
- Show/hide features based on role
- Use `RoleBasedNavigation.getDestinations()` for navigation

### Multi-Child Support
- Parents can switch between children using `ref.read(authProvider.notifier).switchToChild(child)`
- Check `ref.watch(hasSelectedChildProvider)` to see if viewing child context
- Use `ref.watch(selectedChildProvider)` to get current child
- Show `ChildContextBar` when parent is viewing child profile

## Environment Configuration

### Environment Usage
- Use `ApiConfig.baseUrl` for API endpoints
- Use `ApiConfig.environmentName` for logging
- Check `ApiConfig.isDevelopment` for debug-only code
- Never hardcode API URLs - use `ApiConfig` constants

## Testing Considerations

### Test Structure
- Place tests in `test/` directory
- Mirror feature structure: `test/features/feature_name/`
- Use descriptive test names
- Test widgets, providers, and business logic separately

## Common Patterns to Follow

### Provider Dependency
```dart
// Good: Provider depends on another provider
final featureProvider = Provider<Feature>((ref) {
  final repository = ref.watch(repositoryProvider);
  return Feature(repository);
});

// Bad: Direct instantiation
final featureProvider = Provider<Feature>((ref) {
  return Feature(Repository()); // Don't do this
});
```

### State Updates
```dart
// Good: Use copyWith for immutable updates
state = state.copyWith(isLoading: true, error: null);

// Bad: Mutating state directly
state.isLoading = true; // Don't do this
```

### Widget Building
```dart
// Good: Extract complex widgets
class _ComplexSection extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) { ... }
}

// Good: Use const where possible
const SizedBox(height: AppSpacing.md)

// Good: Handle all states
data.when(
  data: (value) => ...,
  loading: () => ...,
  error: (error, stack) => ...,
)
```

## What NOT to Do

### Anti-Patterns to Avoid
- ❌ Don't use `print()` - use `AppLogger`
- ❌ Don't create markdown files unless explicitly requested
- ❌ Don't hardcode colors/spacing - use design tokens
- ❌ Don't mutate state directly - use `copyWith`
- ❌ Don't create global variables - use Riverpod providers
- ❌ Don't use `setState` in ConsumerWidget - use Riverpod
- ❌ Don't hardcode API URLs - use `ApiConfig`
- ❌ Don't ignore errors - always handle and log them
- ❌ Don't create features outside the feature structure
- ❌ Don't mix presentation and business logic

## Feature Creation Checklist

When creating a new feature:
1. ✅ Create feature directory in `lib/features/feature_name/`
2. ✅ Create domain model in `domain/` (if needed)
3. ✅ Create repository in `data/` (if needed)
4. ✅ Create service in `lib/core/data/services/` (if API integration)
5. ✅ Create providers in `providers/` or `lib/core/providers/`
6. ✅ Create screen in `presentation/`
7. ✅ Add route to `lib/core/router/app_router.dart`
8. ✅ Use design tokens for styling
9. ✅ Implement loading/error/empty states
10. ✅ Add proper logging
11. ✅ Handle errors gracefully
12. ✅ Use Riverpod for state management
13. ✅ Follow role-based access patterns (if applicable)

## Summary

- **Architecture**: Clean Architecture with Presentation/Domain/Data layers
- **State**: Riverpod with StateNotifier for complex state
- **Navigation**: GoRouter with type-safe routes
- **Models**: Immutable with copyWith, fromJson, toJson
- **Logging**: AppLogger (never print)
- **Errors**: Custom exceptions with user-friendly messages
- **UI**: Design tokens, Material 3, reusable components
- **API**: ApiClient + Services pattern
- **Organization**: Feature-based structure
- **Quality**: Type-safe, null-safe, well-documented
- **Files**: NO markdown files unless explicitly requested

